Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 02:52:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] cuda qmmf: half tiles for decode calls whose 64-column
 grid leaves SMs idle

A 16-row MXFP8 or BF16 lane call whose 64-column grid holds fewer than 96 blocks
(TF_QMMF_HALF_BELOW, 0 never) takes a 32-column tile: half a packed tile's n8 tiles, contiguous
in both formats' word and scale order, so each column's mma chain, K slices and cluster sum are
the 64-column tile's and its bits the same, with twice the blocks. Flash Next's hyper-connection
down projection ([324, 10240] BF16, 6 tiles x 8 slices) reads its weights at 183 GB/s instead of
158 (36.3 vs 42.0 us cold), the router and the shared expert's MXFP8 projections 4-8 % faster.
The existing instances are untouched (their loads stay behind `if constexpr (BN == 64)`).

tf-cuda-test lane-half: MXFP8 and BF16, fp32 and bf16 out, bytes equal to the 64-column tile at
Flash Next's decode shapes and the column edges, FP64-bounded, timed on weights past the L2.
Served (FP4-Local, no drafts): 23.95 / 23.83 tok/s one stream (main 23.58 / 23.66), replies equal.

(cherry picked from commit 8a8a86e3245c112700774abc6b32b00748a511c7)
